{"url": "https://www.brandeis.edu/molecular-cell-biology/graduate/phd-program/faqs.html", "final_url": "https://www.brandeis.edu/molecular-cell-biology/graduate/phd-program/faqs.html", "status": 200, "method": "http", "fetched": "2026-11-12", "text": "FAQs for PhD Program | PhD Program | Graduate Studies | Molecular and Cell Biology Program | Brandeis University\nSkip to content\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch this site\nSearch Brandeis.edu\nSearch\nMenu\nFaculty & Research\nGraduate Studies\nPhD Program\nApply to the PhD Program\nTraining\nFunding\nTeaching & Mentoring\nCross-disciplinary MCB\nFAQs for PhD Program\nWhere Are They Now?\nPhD Handbook\nMaster's Program\nApply to the Master's Program\nMaster's Program FAQs\nMS Handbook\nResources & Support\nHousing\nGraduate Student Representatives\nFacilities\nEvents\nDepartment Values\nBlack Lives Matter\nContact\nHome\nFaculty & Research\nGraduate Studies\nPhD Program\nApply to the PhD Program\nTraining\nFunding\nTeaching & Mentoring\nCross-disciplinary MCB\nFAQs for PhD Program\nWhere Are They Now?\nPhD Handbook\nMaster's Program\nApply to the Master's Program\nMaster's Program FAQs\nMS Handbook\nResources & Support\nHousing\nGraduate Student Representatives\nFacilities\nEvents\nDepartment Values\nBlack Lives Matter\nContact\nHome\nAcademics\nSchools\nDegree Programs\nBrandeis Online\nSummer Programs\nMicrocredentials\nAdmissions & Aid\nUndergraduate Admissions\nGraduate Admissions\nFinancial Aid\nRabb School of Continuing Studies\nResearch\nCenters and Institutes\nFunding Resources\nAthletics\nStudent Life\nHousing/Community Living\nDining\nClubs and Organizations\nCommunity Service\nAthletics\nArts\nRose Art Museum\nAbout\nOur Story\nOur Jewish Roots\nMission and Diversity Statements\nFast Facts\nVisiting\nAdministration\nVisit\nStudents\nFaculty & Staff\nAlumni & Friends\nParents & Families\nGiving\nPopular Resources\nCampus Calendar\nDirectories\nEmployment\nMoodle\nNews/Media\nShuttle Schedules\nSupport at Brandeis\nWorkday\nSites A-Z\nMolecular and Cell Biology Program\nHome /\nGraduate Studies /\nPhD Program /\nFAQs for PhD Program\nFAQs for PhD Program\nConfocal image of U2OS osteosarcoma cells fixed and stained with antibodies to APC (green), antibodies to vinculin to mark focal adhesions (blue), and phalloidin to mark F-actin (pink). Image: M.Angeles Juanes (Bruce Goode lab).\nAdmissions FAQs Tuition and Financial Aid FAQs\nProgram Requirement FAQs\nExpand All\nHow long does it take to get a PhD degree?\nThe minimum residency requirement is three years, but students generally finish in five to six years.\nWhat are the course requirements for the MCB PhD degree?\nPlease see the University Bulletin for detailed information.\nWhat are the research requirements?\nAll MCB PhD students are expected to create a new body of knowledge relevant to their field. Students will join a laboratory at the end of their first year and formally conduct an original investigation under the guidance and mentorship of their thesis advisor. Students will write a dissertation of their results and are expected to have at least one first-author paper accepted or published in a quality journal at the time of their thesis defense.\nHow do I choose a research lab?\nFaculty members cannot accept graduate students directly into their labs prior to acceptance into the program. All MCB PhD students must complete four research rotations in their first year before choosing a dissertation lab. The choice of dissertation laboratory is made jointly by the student and the faculty member.\nHow do rotations work?\nStudents may choose to rotate with any member of the Life Sciences community. The Life Sciences programs hold a \u201cfaculty bazaar\u201d during orientation week to aid in the rotation selection process. This is a three-night series of short research presentations (with dinner) designed to acquaint students with research opportunities at Brandeis. Students will submit three choices for their first rotation. Subsequent rotations will be jointly arranged between the student and the faculty member in whose lab the rotation is to take place.\nPlease see the University Bulletin for detailed information.\nWhat are the teaching requirements?\nPlease see the University Bulletin for detailed information.\nWhat are the qualifying exam requirements for a PhD in MCB at Brandeis?\nPlease see the University Bulletin for detailed information.\nWhat are the dissertation requirements for a PhD in MCB at Brandeis?\nBeginning in the third year, each student is required to meet at least once per year with their thesis committee to evaluate their dissertation progress. Specific PhD thesis requirements are set by the student\u2019s advisor and the thesis committee. As a rough guideline, a PhD student should have at least one first-author paper accepted or published at the time of their thesis defense. With approval of the thesis committee, the student will write and submit a dissertation of their results. After submission of the dissertation, the candidate will give a public thesis seminar to the University community, and on the same day defend the work in an oral exam to the dissertation committee.\nPlease see the University Bulletin for detailed information.\nFaculty & Research\nGraduate Studies\nPhD Program\nApply to the PhD Program\nTraining\nFunding\nTeaching & Mentoring\nCross-disciplinary MCB\nFAQs for PhD Program\nWhere Are They Now?\nPhD Handbook\nMaster's Program\nResources & Support\nHousing\nGraduate Student Representatives\nFacilities\nEvents\nDepartment Values\nBlack Lives Matter\nContact\nHome\nFind\nScience at Brandeis\nGraduate Admissions\nDo\nRequest Information\nVisit Brandeis\nApply\nContact Us\nMolecular and Cell Biology Program\nSchool of Science, Engineering and Technology Graduate Affairs Office\nRosenstiel-Kosow Connector 3-RK02\nMailstop 009\n415 South Street\nWaltham, MA 02454-9110\n781-736-2352\nscigradoffice@brandeis.edu\nHours: 9 a.m. - 4:30 p.m\nConnect Wit